<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chp-conditional-flow" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Conditional Flow</title>

	<introduction>

		<p>
			So far, we've written code that runs step by step in a straight line. Real programs
			need to react to data or user input, choosing different actions when conditions change.
		</p>

		<p>
			For example, suppose <pf>gift_card_balance</pf> stores the amount of money on an Amazon
			gift card and <pf>total_due</pf> stores the price of a purchase. Before completing the
			purchase, your program needs to ask a yes-or-no question:
			<q>Is <pf>gift_card_balance</pf> greater than or equal to <pf>total_due</pf>?</q>
		</p>

		<p>
			Questions like this are called <term>logical expressions</term>, and their answers are
			called <term>logical values</term> (i.e., <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>). Two types of
			structures that let you ask questions and make decisions based on their answers are
			<pf>if</pf>-statements and <pf>switch</pf>-statements. These structures allow you to
			control the flow of your program, executing different blocks of code depending on the
			conditions you specify.
		</p>

		<p>
			In this section, we will learn how MATLAB evaluates logical expressions and how to use <pf>if</pf> and <pf>switch</pf> statements to create programs that can make decisions and respond to different situations.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<xi:include href="sec-logical-expressions.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="sec-if-statement.ptx" /> 
	<xi:include href="sec-switch-statement.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="exercises-conditional-flow.ptx" />

	<conclusion>
		<title>🗝️ Key Takeaways...</title>
		<assemblage xml:id="takeaways-conditional-flow">
			<p>
				<ul marker="square">
					<li>
						<term>Logical values</term>.
						MATLAB has two special values for yes/no answers: <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>.
						These are of type <pf>logical</pf>, which is different from the numeric type <pf>double</pf>.
					</li>
					<li>
						<term>Logical Expressions</term>.
						A logical expression is a command that evaluates to true or false.
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><term>Relational operators</term>. The relational operators are:</p>
						<tabular>
							<row><cell><pf>==</pf></cell><cell>equal to</cell><cell><pf>~=</pf></cell><cell>not equal to</cell></row>
							<row><cell><pf>&lt;</pf></cell><cell>less than</cell><cell><pf>&lt;=</pf></cell><cell>less than or equal to</cell></row>
							<row><cell><pf>&gt;</pf></cell><cell>greater than</cell><cell><pf>&gt;=</pf></cell><cell>greater than or equal to</cell></row>
						</tabular>
					</li>
					<li>
						<term>Single equals vs. double equals</term>.
						A single equals (<pf>=</pf>) assigns a value to a variable, while a double equals (<pf>==</pf>) compares two values.
					</li>
					<li>
						<term>The NOT operator</term>.
						The tilde (<pf>~</pf>) flips <pf>true</pf> to <pf>false</pf> and vice versa. For example, <pf>~(x == y)</pf> is equivalent to <pf>x ~= y</pf>.
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>Combining logical expressions</term>.
							Use <pf>&amp;</pf> for AND (both must be true) and <pf>|</pf> for OR (at least one must be true). For example:
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								(age &gt;= 18) &amp; hasLicense  % true only if both are true
								(x &lt; 0) | (x &gt; 100)       % true if either is true
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<term>Operator precedence</term>.
						When combining logical operators, NOT (<pf>~</pf>) is evaluated first, then AND (<pf>&amp;</pf>), then OR (<pf>|</pf>). Use parentheses to clarify complex expressions.
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><term>If statement</term>.</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								if condA
									blockA	% ⬅ runs if condA is true
								elseif condB
									blockB	% ⬅ runs if condB is true and condA is false
								else
									blockC	% ⬅ runs if condA and condB are both false
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><term>Switch statement</term>.</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								switch var
									case valueA
										blockA	% ⬅ runs if var matches valueA exactly
									case valueB
										blockB	% ⬅ runs if var matches valueB exactly
									otherwise
										blockC	% ⬅ runs if no cases above match
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>If vs. Switch</term>.
							Use <pf>switch</pf> to compare one variable against a small set of exact values (especially strings or integers).
							Use <pf>if</pf> when your conditions involve ranges, inequalities, or multiple variables.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</conclusion>

</chapter>